   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to including certain\ninformation about seeking extreme risk protection orders in written service\nplans for discharge and conditional release of patients to the community",
    "basePrintNo": "S5434",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5434-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 5,
       "fullName": "Jim Gaughran",
       "imgName": "1042_Jim_Gaughran.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1222,
       "sessionMemberId": 1807,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "GAUGHRAN"
      },
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 37,
       "fullName": "Shelley Mayer",
       "imgName": "623_shelley_mayer.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1143,
       "sessionMemberId": 1824,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "MAYER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 2
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §29.15, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5434\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to including certain\ninformation about seeking extreme risk protection orders in written\nservice plans for discharge and conditional release of patients to the\ncommunity\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe bill will supply patients and their authorized representative with\nmaterials providing information related to seeking an extreme risk\nprotection order prior to a patient's discharge or conditional release\nfrom a mental health facility.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends paragraph 5 of subdivision (g) of section 29.15 of\nthe mental hygiene law to add a new paragraph 6, which would require\nmental health facilities to give material providing information related\nto seeking extreme risk protection orders to a patient and an authorized\nrepresentative who has actively participated in the development of a\nplan for such patient's discharge or conditional release, prior to the\npatient's discharge or conditional release.  This material shall also\ninclude factors which a court might consider in determining if grounds\nfor issuing an extreme risk protection order are present.\n\nSection two provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nLast year, the Red Flag Law took effect in New York State. Under this\nlaw, if an individual is deemed to be a threat and an extreme risk\nprotection court order is issued, law enforcement is then authorized to\nconfiscate any firearms the individual is in possession of, and the\norder also prevents the individual from purchasing a firearm while the\norder is in place. The Red Flag Law was put into effect with the hopes\nof preventing mass shootings, domestic incidents and suicides among\nother tragedies, all of which have continued to devastate our country.\n\nIn the summer of 2019, a Bronxville woman who had voluntarily checked\nherself into a mental health facility left the institution after being\nofficially discharged by the hospital. Upon her departure from the\nfacility, the woman purchased a firearm and ultimately committed suicide\nshortly after.\n\nAfter the woman's death, her husband contacted local officials not\nunderstanding why his wife was able to purchase a firearm in the first\nplace, having been recently hospitalized. Her family did not have previ-\nous knowledge of the Red Flag Law and her husband stated that if he had\nknown about extreme risk protection orders, he would have tried to\nsecure an order for his wife. This bill provides that prior to a\npatient's discharge or conditional release from a mental health facili-\nty, the patient and their authorized representative who has been\ninvolved with their medical care, will automatically receive materials\ninforming them of the Red Flag Law. Providing such notice will prevent\nfuture tragedies and will ensure that patients and their families are\nmade aware of the options that are available if they have concerns about\nthe safety of their loved ones.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.10716, 2020, referred to mental health\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
