    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5434A\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to including certain\ninformation about extreme risk protection orders in written service\nplans for discharge and conditional release of patients to the community\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe bill will supply patients and their authorized representative with\nmaterials providing information related to seeking an extreme risk\nprotection order prior to a patient's discharge or conditional release\nfrom a mental health facility.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends paragraph 5 of subdivision (g) of section 29.15 of\nthe mental hygiene law to add a new paragraph 6, which would require\nmental health facilities to give material providing information related\nto extreme risk protection orders to a patient or, upon request of the\npatient, an authorized representative who has actively participated in\nthe patient's treatment plan.\n\nSection two provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2019, the Red Flag Law took effect in New York State. Under this law,\nif an individual is deemed to be a threat and an extreme risk protection\ncourt order is issued, law enforcement is then authorized to confiscate\nany firearms the individual is in possession of, and the order also\nprevents the individual from purchasing a firearm while the order is in\nplace. The Red Flag Law was put into effect with the hopes of preventing\nmass shootings, domestic incidents and suicides among other tragedies,\nall of which have continued to devastate our country.  In the summer of\n2019, a Bronxville woman who had voluntarily checked herself into a\nmental health facility left the institution after being officially\ndischarged by the hospital. Upon her departure from the facility, the\nwoman purchased a firearm and ultimately committed suicide shortly\nafter.\n\nAfter the woman's death, her husband contacted local officials not\nunderstanding why his wife was able to purchase a firearm in the first\nplace, having been recently hospitalized. Her family did not have previ-\nous knowledge of the Red Flag Law and her husband stated that if he had\nknown about extreme risk protection orders, he would have tried to\nsecure an order for his wife. This bill provides that prior to a\npatient's discharge or conditional release from a mental health facili-\nty, the patient or, upon request of the patient, their authorized repre-\nsentative who has been involved with their medical care, will receive\nmaterials informing them of the Red Flag Law.\n\nProviding such notice will prevent future tragedies and will ensure that\npatients and their families are made aware of the options that are\navailable if they have concerns about the safety of their loved ones.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nA.10716, 2020, referred to mental health\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
